A||||8||Formula 18||3800 5-speed   terryk ||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||01-08-2001||12:36 PM||||Hey guys, <BR>What problems can be expected using the 5-speed on a 3800. I have read that the ODBII don't like wot shifts b/c of a torque managment problem but what about the earlier ones? It will probllay throw some codes but anyone know the fixes (terryK) I am thinking about using a 92 SC then upgrading to a 95 blower with the 92 pulley when my pocket recovers from stage I. <P>Any imput???<P><P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||01-08-2001||02:18 PM||||I agree that the SC code doesn't like manual transmissions much. There is no stall-saver code and there is tons of transmission monitoring and protection. The results is some of the problems people talk about: stalling, temporary power loss, etc.<P>If you use a 92/93 or 94/95 ECM, I can do whatever is need to clear the codes and remove the shift torque (although that might not be a good idea on a Getrag.) Faking stall-saver might be a big task but if it's run in 1st gear mode with no ABM, it should be ok.  Either way, it would work better than disconnecting the SES lamp.<P>Let me know if you want some help.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-08-2001||02:48 PM||||I'm running the '97 Buick ECM with my series II SC 5 sp no probs other than I do have a constant engine light(removed bulb) ECM thinks the auto tranny is slipping, but no power losses or stalling probs. I don't drive it hard either. What you might consider a prob is that the rev limiter is set at 5400 and that comes up quite quickly and a 120 MPH speed limiter.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pollock||01-08-2001||10:14 PM||||Terry, what do you mean by shift torque?  Also, what does ABM stand for?<P>Anyone with an ACE kit, do you have these problems?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-08-2001||10:15 PM||||Mine was done by ACE.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-08-2001||10:45 PM||||Shift torque is a reduction in engine power during shifts. The 4T60E/65E can't take the full power of the 3800 SC and remain reliable to the level GM wants. When a shift is ready to occur, the ECM backs off the timing, performance the shift, and advances it again (simplification a bit). How much is dependent on RPM and engine load (what are called "excess torque counts"). Third gear is really backed-off.<P>ABM is Abusive Maneuver. This is a protection against park/neutral drops and full throttle starts. A good example is:<P>1. MPH is 0<BR>2. Throttle is at 20% TPS<BR>3. The brake is pressed<BR>4. The trans is in first. <P>Gee, I wonder what he's thinking.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>TK<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||01-08-2001||10:56 PM||||Terry- does the ECM and tranny in the Northstar Caddy's work like that too? I drove my mom's a while back and it would roast the tires from a "foot off the brake" start, but when you hold it back against the converter with the brakes, it was a real slug off the line.||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||01-08-2001||11:19 PM||||I've only looked at the NS code briefly, but I'm sure it has the same stuff. ABM will kick in differently depending on what you are doing. <P>For example, in the 92/93 SC, the ABM is active until 10MPH, above 3750 RPM, above 30% TPS, stays in ABM is the TPS stays above 11.7% and for .5 seconds minimum. If you jam it to the floor from a stop with the brakes off, it will tend to kick out earlier than if you power-jacked it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||pollock||01-10-2001||12:11 AM||||Thanks, guys.  I want one of these motors in my car so bad, I can't stand it.  <P>Anybody know what last night's lottery numbers were?||||reg||1
